\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
A Short Review Of The 1972 Freedman-Clauser And 1982 Aspect Experiments:
 An Empirical Mapping of Continuous Attenuation Functions vs.
 Discrete Binary Products.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freedman's thesis 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 thesis describes a measurement of the polarization correlation of the two photons emitted in the 
\begin_inset Formula $4p^{2}\;^{1}S_{0}\rightarrow4s4p^{1}P_{1}\rightarrow4s^{2}\;^{1}S_{0}$
\end_inset

 cascade of calcium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Ideal Two-Photon System
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The system that is investigated by Freedman's experiment 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "Section D The Ideal Two-Photon System"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 consists of two distinguishable photons,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 emitted in an atomic cascade.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To a good approximation,
 the atom cascades via electric dipole transitions through angular-momentum states 
\begin_inset Formula $J=0\rightarrow J=1\rightarrow J=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The most ·general state of this kind is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left|\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\right\rangle  & = & a\left|R_{+}\right\rangle _{1}\left|L_{+}\right\rangle _{2}+b\left|L_{+}\right\rangle _{1}\left|R_{+}\right\rangle _{2}+c\left|R_{-}\right\rangle _{1}\left|L_{-}\right\rangle _{2}+d\left|L_{-}\right\rangle _{1}\left|R_{-}\right\rangle _{2}\\
 &  & +e\left|R_{+}\right\rangle _{1}\left|R_{-}\right\rangle _{2}+f\left|L_{+}\right\rangle _{1}\left|L_{-}\right\rangle _{2}+g\left|R_{-}\right\rangle _{1}\left|R_{+}\right\rangle _{2}+h\left|L_{-}\right\rangle _{1}\left|L_{+}\right\rangle _{2}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\left|R_{+}\right\rangle _{1}$
\end_inset

 is the eigenstate of a right-hand circularly polarized photon traveling along the +z axis.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The subscripts label the photons which are distinguishable by their energies.where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda_{2}$
\end_inset

 is the space from which 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

 is chosen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 represent the pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freedman incorrectly states that the expectation value for the product of two individual spin measurements 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left(\hat{a},\lambda_{1}\right)B\left(\hat{b},\lambda_{2}\right)=\pm$
\end_inset

1 in the directions 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{b}$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "equation (I.2)"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right) & = & \left\langle \psi\left|\hat{a}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{1}}\,\hat{b}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{2}}\right|\psi\right\rangle =-\hat{a}\cdot\hat{b}\label{eq:FI.2}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Experimental Descritions 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experiments of Freedman 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "Section F. Outline of the Experiment"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and Aspect are similar.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both experiments photons are emitted in an atomic cascade and are counted in coincidence,
 the coincidence rate being observed as a function of the individual photon polarization states.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following quantities are measured:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R\left(\phi\right)$
\end_inset

:
 the coincidence rate for two-photon detection as a function of the angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 between the planes of linear polarization defined by the orientation of the two inserted polarizers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R_{1}$
\end_inset

:
 the coincidence rate with polarizer 2 removed;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R_{2}$
\end_inset

:
 the coincidence rate with polarizer 1 removed;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R_{0}$
\end_inset

:
 the coincidence rate with both polarizers removed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These rates are determined by correcting the observed coincidence rates for the random coincidence background.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to ordinary coincidence counting,
 a time delay-coincidence system provides a spectrum of the arrival time difference between 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{2}$
\end_inset

 This gives the lifetime of the 
\begin_inset Formula $4s4p^{1}P_{1}$
\end_inset

 state and provides a check on the consistency of the experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Application of the Generalized Inequality to the Present Experiment
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "Section?B"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the experiment,
 the particle pairs are pairs of photons emitted in a 
\begin_inset Formula $0\rightarrow1\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 atomic cascade;
 the measuring devices each consist of a linear polarizer and a photomultiplier tube.
 The adjustable parameter of each measuring device is the orientation of the polarizer about the z axis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It would be natural to associate the results of a measurement an each photon with passage in either the ordinary ray or the extraordinary ray of the polarizer but in any real polarizer some photons are not transmitted in either ray because of scattering losses or absorption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 there are three possible results for a measurement that apparently violating the condition that measurements be two-valued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 we are free to associate 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 with the transmission in the ordinary ray and
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 with non-transmission in the ordinary ray.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This interpretation makes the measurements two-valued and has the practical advantage of making it unnecessary to detect photons in the extra-ordinary ray.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To express inequalities II.4 in terms of quantities directly measured,
 it is convenient to introduce the following partition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Lambda_{ij}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right) & = & \left\{ \lambda|A\left(\hat{a},\lambda\right)=i\,and\,B\left(\hat{b},\lambda\right)=j\right\} 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 equal 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using this partition,
 define:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P_{ij}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right) & = & \int_{\Lambda_{ij}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)}\rho\left(\lambda\right)d\lambda
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $P_{++}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)$
\end_inset

 being the probability of joint passage of the photons through the polarizers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:FI.2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right) & = & P_{++}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)-P_{+-}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)-P_{-+}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)+P_{--}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Define:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P_{1}\left(\hat{a}\right) & = & P_{++}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)+P_{+-}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)\\
P_{2}\left(\hat{b}\right) & = & P_{++}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)+P_{-+}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $P_{1}\left(\hat{a}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{2}\left(\hat{b}\right)$
\end_inset

 being the probabilities of joint passage when one of the polarizers is absent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Expressing the correlation ftmction in terms of the probabilities of joint passage:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right) & = & 4P_{++}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)-2P_{1}\left(\hat{a}\right)-2P_{2}\left(\hat{b}\right)+1\label{eq:F1.5}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freedman derived the inequalities :
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
-2\leq & P\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)-P\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)+P\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)+P\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right) & \leq2\label{eq:FII.4}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On substitution of Equation
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:F1.5"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 into the inequalities 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:FII.4"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "equation (II.4)"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
-1\leq & P_{++}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)-P_{++}\left(\hat{a},\hat{c}\right)+P_{++}\left(\hat{d},\hat{c}\right)+P_{++}\left(\hat{d},\hat{b}\right)-P_{2}\left(\hat{b}\right)-P_{1}\left(\hat{d}\right) & \leq0\label{eq:FII.6}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If all transmitted photons are detected:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P_{++}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right) & = & \frac{R\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)}{R_{0}}\label{eq:FII.7a}\\
P_{1}\left(\hat{a}\right) & = & \frac{R_{1}\left(\hat{a}\right)}{R_{0}}\label{eq:FII.7b}\\
P_{2}\left(\hat{b}\right) & = & \frac{R_{2}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)}{R_{0}}\label{eq:FII.7c}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right),R_{1}\left(\hat{a}\right),R_{2}\left(\hat{b}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{0}$
\end_inset

 are the coincidence count rates.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{R}{R_{0}}$
\end_inset

 is the fraction of individual photon pairs that successfully pass through a polariser and can be considered as an individual event probability (a fraction).
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{2}$
\end_inset

 represent coincidence rates with polarizer removed,
 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{1}\left(\hat{a}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{2}\left(\hat{b}\right)$
\end_inset

 are independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{b}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)$
\end_inset

 depends only 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}\cdot\hat{b}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
-1\leq & \frac{R\left(\phi_{1}\right)}{R_{0}}-\frac{R\left(\phi_{1}+\phi_{2}+\phi_{3}\right)}{R_{0}}+\frac{R\left(\phi_{2}\right)}{R_{0}}+\frac{R\left(\phi_{3}\right)}{R_{0}}-\frac{R_{1}}{R_{0}}-\frac{R_{2}}{R_{0}} & \leq0\label{eq:FII.8}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\phi_{1} & = & cos^{-1}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)\\
\phi_{1} & = & cos^{-1}\left(\hat{d},\hat{c}\right)\\
\phi_{1} & = & cos^{-1}\left(\hat{d},\hat{b}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\phi_{1}+\phi_{2}+\phi_{3} & = & cos^{-1}\left(\hat{a},\hat{c}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Neither Freedman's nor Aspect's experiment satisfies the fundamental probability constraints mandated by Bell’s 1964 Equation (2).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both experiments the empirical data points track a smooth,
 solid continuous curve 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\langle R(\phi)/R_{0}\rangle}$
\end_inset

 because the laboratory instruments are measuring continuous macroscopic wave intensities and timing intervals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Continuous profile divergence from standard CHSH inequalities mathematically demonstrates that the inequality is completely inapplicable to this class of data;
 not that nature has violated local realistic probability logic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
\start_of_appendix
Appendices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Click rate measurement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The Clicks Enter:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Photons hit Detector A and Detector B each of which converts the light into an electrical pulse—
a raw "click".
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The Gating Circuit:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

These pulses travel down coaxial cables into a coincidence circuit which acts like a logical AND gate with a built-in stopwatch.
 If pulse A and pulse B arrive within about 5 to 30 nanoseconds of each other the circuit emits a single "coincidence pulse".
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The Counter (The Scaler):
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The coincidence pulse travels into an electronic counter (historically called a scaler).
 It does not calculate rates dynamically;
 it just counts.
 It ticks up:
 1,
 2,
 3,
 4...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Reporting the Rate:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

To get the rate a mechanical timer collects clicks for a fixed period of time (say,
 100 seconds).
 If the counter reads 2,400 when the timer went off the reported rate for that run is written down as 24 clicks per second.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Effectively,
 the device counts individual,
 discrete events over a fixed timeframe and that total count is converted into a "rate" because it is an easy way to write down the data and normalize it against baseline data 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(R_{0}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

